THE AUTHORSHIP-AND-CELL SEAM — SPECIFIED, NOT CUT.
NOT APPLIED to this tree. Pass-1 research lanes are read-only on product files; this is the
patch a synthesizer hands to W1's file, written out so the next pass argues with code rather
than with prose. Line numbers are HEAD at 2026-09-17 (`useGameState.ts` 1,2xx lines).

WHY IT IS NEEDED. `applyCellValue` is the one primitive every digit passes through — a
keystroke, an undo replay, and a PEER's move, which arrives at `useGameState.ts:375`
(`sessionSource.applyValue` → `applyCellValue`) from `useSession.ts:792`. Two lines inside it
null the margin's two graphite/red voices:

    487:  hintReasoning.value = null;   // T4-W7 — an edit disarms a stale armed hint
    488:  lastRefusal.value  = null;    // T9-W1 §1.1 — ink that LANDS retracts the refusal

Neither line knows whose hand wrote the digit, so a peer typing in the opposite corner takes
YOUR hint note and YOUR refusal note off your page. Measured, both engines, two live pages on
`?wire=local`: `logs/e4-peer-*.json` — a peer's digit elsewhere leaves `""` where
"only 6 fits here" was (chromium) / "only 5 fits here" (webkit). A JOIN leaves the note
standing, because a join touches no value.

THE RULE THE FAMILY WANTS. Your own write always retracts (unchanged). A peer's write retracts
only when it changes what the note SAID — the cell the note names, or a cell its argument
turns on. A peer's write anywhere else leaves it standing, and a peer's write never touches
your refusal at all (a refusal is about YOUR keystroke on a given, and no peer can write a
given either).

